    "chunk_index": 82,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "59db65da0ba008558e0a1dd56433a813",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "The allocation of Rs. 11,000 million for improving the facilities and infrastructure of the medical faculties of the Sabaragamuwa, Ruhuna, Uva Wellassa and Eastern Universities is a move in the right direction. Then, the Proposal in the Budget, 2026 to increase the Mahapola allowance by a further Rs. 2,500 to what was granted in 2025, taking the total amount of what they would receive to Rs. 10,000 monthly is a great boost to our future generation of graduates and the educated class of this country. These, Sir, are long-term commitments that would shape the next generation of Sri Lanka.\n\nAlso, the assistance that will be granted to Thalassemic patients is commendable. Thalassemic patients - Sir, you know very well - are those who are born to Thalassemic minor parents. When both parents are Thalassemic minor, they have a 25 per cent chance of having a Thalassemic major baby. It is a big burden even for a very well-to-do family. I know a couple of well-to-\n\ndo Thalassemic families, whose children, with the best of care, could not survive beyond 25 years of age. Among our general population, their survival is nothing more than 13 to 15 years. They have to have chelation therapy to remove excess iron. At least five days a week, they have to have regular blood transfusions. So, this Budget has made provision for a grant of Rs. 10,000 for a deserving family who have Thalassemic children. I would personally suggest to this Legislature that we make genetic screening of susceptible population in this country mandatory and send them for counselling, in case both parents are found to be Thalassemic minor. However, I would refrain from saying that they should be discouraged from getting married, but they should be educated about the risks and no legal provision should be brought in at the moment to prevent their marriage."
  },
