    "chunk_size_bytes": 1803,
    "chunk_text": "do Thalassemic families, whose children, with the best of care, could not survive beyond 25 years of age. Among our general population, their survival is nothing more than 13 to 15 years. They have to have chelation therapy to remove excess iron. At least five days a week, they have to have regular blood transfusions. So, this Budget has made provision for a grant of Rs. 10,000 for a deserving family who have Thalassemic children. I would personally suggest to this Legislature that we make genetic screening of susceptible population in this country mandatory and send them for counselling, in case both parents are found to be Thalassemic minor. However, I would refrain from saying that they should be discouraged from getting married, but they should be educated about the risks and no legal provision should be brought in at the moment to prevent their marriage.\n\nSir, beyond these sectors, the Budget reflects renewed commitment to inclusive growth, ensuring that its benefits reach every region, every community and every family. The focus on SMEs, digital infrastructure and agricultural modernization shows that we are preparing the ground for an economy that empowers entrepreneurs, encourages innovation and values productivity.\n\nSir, fiscal credibility is as important as fiscal content. The international community has acknowledged that Sri Lanka has begun to restore trust through responsible governance. We must ensure - and, it is certain - that every rupee allocated reaches its intended purpose, whether it be for a school in Monaragala, a hospital in Jaffna or a housing complex in Puttalam.\n\nSir, the Budget, 2026 is not the end of reform; it is a continuation of nation rebuilding. It provides the framework for a resilient economy, a healthier nation and a disciplined society."
  },
