    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "2e53586e7424b80c5f3db933bcc143d1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1914,
    "chunk_text": "\"Sri Lanka emphasizes the need for restraint and to be committed to diplomatic dialogue \u2026\"\n\nWhat is this meaningless statement? Why are you issuing a statement like this? Rather than issuing a statement of this nature, you could have just kept quiet.\n\nA few days ago, one British journalist in the US was arrested for having some meetings, condemning the actions of Israel in the United States. Similarly, you are doing the same thing here, summoning whoever who makes a comment in that regard. Only a few months ago, an innocent young man who posted a sticker showing his solidarity with Palestinians was summoned to the TID. Now, the TID has become a tool of the Israelis in this country. Is this the way you are treating innocent citizens in the country who show their outrage for the actions taken by Israelis in the Occupied Territories of Palestine? So, that must stop.\n\nThe Government has promised in Geneva to abolish the PTA. But, what has happened? Today, I saw a statement by the Attorney-General\u2019s Department that they are opposed to creating an independent Public Prosecutor's Office. - [Interruption.] No. You can reply after I comment. An independent Prosecutor's Office is an assurance the Government had given in Geneva to the international community. We know how the Attorney-General\u2019s Department was being used for political purposes. But, today, the Attorney-General\u2019s Department is up in arms issuing a statement, condemning or rather asking the Government to review this situation. Particularly, the Attorney-General\u2019s Department has been complicit in many politically-motivated prosecutions. The President, several others and I were summoned to a special Presidential Commission, where the Attorney-General\u2019s Department came for prosecution, and the result would have been the removal of our civic rights. The Attorney-General\u2019s Department was used to conduct the prosecutions."
  },
