    "chunk_size_bytes": 1623,
    "chunk_text": "[4.13 p.m.] \u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0da2\u0dd3.\u0da2\u0dd3. \u0db1\u0db4\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dca\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbd\u0db8\u0dca \u0dd9\u0dad\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bc8\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bc8\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0bc8\u0bc0.\u0bc8\u0bc0. \u0ba4\u0baa\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0ba9\u0bcd\u0ba9\u0bae\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bb2\u0bae\u0bcd) (The Hon. G.G. Ponnambalam) Hon. Presiding Member, I wish to commence my speech today by making reference to the incidents that took place in Trincomalee last evening and this morning. I think it is relevant to the Ministry of Justice and National Integration and I want to make it relevant to that Ministry.\n\nNow, it is in that context, Sir, that I wish to properly put forward the Trincomalee incident. The Tamil population of Trincomalee in 1827 - it was the first census done - was 74.52 per cent, the Muslim population was 24.72 per cent and the Sinhala population was 0.53 per cent. Then, if you take the population just before Independence, in 1946, the census shows that the Tamil population of Trincomalee was 48.74 per cent, the Muslim population was 39.6 per cent and the Sinhala population was 9.87 per cent. That increase in the Sinhala population was because, due to World War II, the Eastern Naval Command was set up in Trincomalee and a significant Sinhala population was brought in to strengthen the harbour and the naval command. But, Sir, if you take the census of today, the Sinhala population of Trincomalee is 26.97 per cent. Today, the Tamil people talk in terms of an ethnic conflict because of the deliberate State-sponsored manoeuvres that have been systematically carried out over the last 76 years to make the Tamil-speaking people a minority in their own areas of historical habitation. That is the crux of the problem."
