    "chunk_size_bytes": 1701,
    "chunk_text": "Now, it is in that context, Sir, that I wish to properly put forward the Trincomalee incident. The Tamil population of Trincomalee in 1827 - it was the first census done - was 74.52 per cent, the Muslim population was 24.72 per cent and the Sinhala population was 0.53 per cent. Then, if you take the population just before Independence, in 1946, the census shows that the Tamil population of Trincomalee was 48.74 per cent, the Muslim population was 39.6 per cent and the Sinhala population was 9.87 per cent. That increase in the Sinhala population was because, due to World War II, the Eastern Naval Command was set up in Trincomalee and a significant Sinhala population was brought in to strengthen the harbour and the naval command. But, Sir, if you take the census of today, the Sinhala population of Trincomalee is 26.97 per cent. Today, the Tamil people talk in terms of an ethnic conflict because of the deliberate State-sponsored manoeuvres that have been systematically carried out over the last 76 years to make the Tamil-speaking people a minority in their own areas of historical habitation. That is the crux of the problem.\n\n351 352\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db8\u0dd4\u0db1\u0dd3\u0dbb\u0dca \u0db8\u0dd4\u0d82\u0d9a \u0dbb\u0dca \u0dca\u0dc4\u0dad\u0d9a]\n\n2025 \u0daf\u0db1\u0d9a\u0dc0\u0dd0\u0dda\u0db4\u0dbb\u0dca 17\n\nIt is in that backdrop, Sir, that the Hon. Minister Ananda Wijepala was approached by several people and to his credit, whatever decisions that were taken yesterday were correct. That is what your Government came to power for, to stand by the law and ensure that there is no racism, but you backtracked. And today, that same Minister shamefully comes to this House, lies and says that the decisions taken yesterday were purely to protect that statue."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
